Vostok 6 launched from Baikonur in what is now Kazakhstan. Over the radio, Tereshkova used the call sign *Chaika*, meaning seagull. Her famous words at launch were "Hey, sky, take off your hat!"

She was in space for about 71 hours. At the time, that was more than all the American astronauts' flights added together. She kept a log, took photographs of the horizon that scientists later used to study the [[atmosphere|atmosphere]], and tested how the body coped with weightlessness. She also felt sick and uncomfortable, crammed into a capsule smaller than an autorickshaw. Her mission overlapped with another Vostok flown by a male cosmonaut, and the two capsules came within about 5 km of each other.

{{story:space-race}}

Those tallies have helped convince cities and countries to act. When clean-ups keep finding the same plastic bags or straws, it becomes easier to argue for banning them. India banned many single-use plastic items, such as plastic straws and cutlery, in July 2022. Several US states have bottle deposits that pay people to return empty drink bottles.

The data also shows what clean-ups can't fix. Tiny pieces of plastic, called [[microplastic|microplastics]], are too small to pick up and are now found from the deepest trenches to Arctic ice.

{{story:recycling}}

The most famous story happened on 5 December 1945. Five US Navy torpedo bombers, called Flight 19, took off from Florida on a training flight. The flight leader became confused, believing his compass was faulty and that they were over the wrong islands. The planes flew back and forth as the weather worsened and their fuel ran low.

They were never found. Most likely, they ran out of fuel and ditched into a rough, dark sea. A rescue flying boat sent after them also vanished. A passing ship reported seeing flames and an oily patch on the water. Planes of that type were known as "flying gas tanks", because fuel fumes could explode.

{{story:mystery-mary-celeste}}

People tried for decades to build a machine that sewed. In 1830, a French tailor named Barthélemy Thimonnier patented a machine that made a simple chain of loops. The French army ordered uniforms from his workshop. Then a crowd of tailors, afraid for their jobs, broke in and smashed the machines.

In the USA, Walter Hunt built a lockstitch machine in the 1830s but never patented it. Elias Howe patented his version in 1846. A few years later Isaac Singer built a more practical machine with a needle that moved straight up and down and a foot pedal to drive it. Howe sued Singer and others for copying his ideas. In the end, the makers agreed to share their [[@patents|patents]] and pay each other fees.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

European harmoniums were big, with foot pedals for the bellows. They suited a church or a parlour, but Indian musicians usually sit on the floor. In 1875, Dwarkanath Ghose of the firm Dwarkin and Son in Kolkata is credited with building a smaller version pumped by hand.

That change made all the difference. A singer could now sit cross-legged, play with the right hand and pump with the left. Harmoniums spread from [[@kolkata|Kolkata]] to temples, theatres and homes across the country. Before long they were a fixture of [[@qawwali|qawwali]] singing at Sufi shrines.

{{story:amritsar}}

Traditionally, painters made their own colours. Black came from soot or burnt rice. Yellow came from [[@turmeric|turmeric]] or [[pollen|pollen]]. Red came from flowers or a red powder, and white from rice paste. Green came from leaves.

Instead of [[@art-materials|bought brushes]], painters used twigs, bamboo sticks, cotton wrapped on sticks and even fingers. Today, many artists use pens, paper and bought paints, but they keep the old style: a strong double outline, and no empty space left blank.

{{story:turmeric}}

In cricket, a batter's average is the total runs scored divided by the number of times they were out. If you score 50, 0 and 100 and get out every time, your average is 150 divided by 3, which is 50.

The most famous average in sport belongs to the Australian batter Don Bradman. In 52 Test matches, he averaged 99.94. Most great batters finish their careers somewhere between about 45 and 60. [[@sachin-tendulkar|Sachin Tendulkar]], who played 200 [[@test-cricket|Test matches]], averaged a little under 54. Bradman needed just four runs in his final innings to average exactly 100. He was out for zero.

{{story:test-cricket}}

The rules are simple once you know them. Write the biggest values first and add. Never repeat a letter more than three times in a row in the modern style. Use subtraction only in six pairs: IV (4), IX (9), XL (40), XC (90), CD (400) and CM (900).

So 1994 is M (1000) + CM (900) + XC (90) + IV (4), written MCMXCIV. The biggest number you can write with these basic rules is MMMCMXCIX, which is 3,999. For bigger numbers, Romans sometimes drew a bar over a numeral to multiply it by 1,000.

{{story:colosseum}}

In the 1930s, the Japanese physicist Ukichiro Nakaya grew the first artificial snowflakes in a cold laboratory. He found that the temperature and moisture of the air decide the shape. Near −2 °C, crystals tend to form flat plates. Around −5 °C, they grow as needles and hollow columns. Near −15 °C in damp air, they grow the classic stars with fern-like arms.

As a flake falls, it passes through layers of different air, and each layer adds its own style. So a snowflake's shape records its journey, like a diary written in ice. Nakaya called snow "a letter sent from heaven".

