The Beatles arrived in Rishikesh in February 1968 with their wives and partners. The ashram sat on a hillside above the Ganga, with stone cottages among the trees. Other famous visitors joined them, including the folk singer Donovan, a member of the Beach Boys, and the actor Mia Farrow and her sister Prudence.

They meditated for hours, attended lectures, and ate simple vegetarian food. Prudence Farrow meditated so much, hardly leaving her room, that John Lennon wrote a song asking her to come out and play: "Dear Prudence".

{{story:sitar-strings}}

Phalke watched a film about the life of Jesus in a Bombay hall around 1910. He came out wondering why India did not make films about its own gods and heroes. He travelled to London to buy a camera and learn the trade.

Money was always short. Phalke borrowed, and his wife Saraswatibai sold her jewellery to help pay for the film. She also worked behind the scenes, developing film and cooking for the cast. When no woman would play the queen, a young man named Anna Salunke took the role. *Raja Harishchandra* opened in May 1913 and was a hit.

{{story:cameras-how}}

Every cell in your body uses oxygen and makes [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]]. When you hold your breath, carbon dioxide piles up in your [[@blood|blood]]. [[sensor|Sensors]] near your brain notice it and push you, harder and harder, to breathe. That burning urge is a warning light, and it switches on long before oxygen runs low.

Freedivers learn to stay calm through the discomfort, because panic burns oxygen faster. Their training is also what makes the sport dangerous. If the warning is ignored for too long, the brain runs short of oxygen and a person can faint without feeling it coming.

{{story:blue-whale}}

The outside of a tooth is coated in [[enamel|enamel]], the hardest substance in your body. It's even harder than [[@bones|bone]]. Under it is a slightly softer, yellowish layer called dentine. In the middle is the pulp, with nerves and blood vessels. That's the part that hurts if a tooth is damaged.

Enamel has no living cells, so it can't repair itself. [[bacteria|Bacteria]] in your mouth feed on [[@sugar-teeth|sugar]] and make [[acid|acid]], which can dissolve tiny holes in it. Brushing twice a day with a [[@made-toothpaste|fluoride toothpaste]] and a good [[@toothbrush|toothbrush]] protects it.

{{story:sharks-old}}

In Cambodia, the temples of Angkor were partly covered by forest after the Khmer capital moved away. Monks still used Angkor Wat. A French naturalist, Henri Mouhot, wrote about it in the 1860s, and his books made it famous in Europe.

{{story:angkor-wat}}

In Central America, Maya cities with stone pyramids were swallowed by rainforest. Exploring them on foot was slow. In 2018, researchers used lidar, [[@lasers|lasers]] fired from aircraft, to map the ground beneath the trees in Guatemala. They found tens of thousands of hidden structures, showing the Maya world was far larger than thought.

In Delhi, [[@mughals|Shah Jahan]], the emperor who built the Taj Mahal, sat on the Peacock Throne. It was covered in gold, rubies, emeralds and pearls, and took about seven years to make. Some accounts say it cost twice as much as the Taj Mahal itself.

In 1739, the Persian ruler Nader Shah invaded Delhi and carried the throne, and the [[@kohinoor|Kohinoor]] diamond, back to Persia. After he was killed in 1747, the throne disappeared, probably broken up for its jewels. Bits of it might be sitting in museums or royal collections today without anyone knowing.

{{story:shipwrecks}}

Most Japanese people follow customs from both Shinto, Japan's own religion with its many shrines, and Buddhism. [[@buddha|Buddhism]] came from India by way of China and [[@seoul|Korea]]. In 752, an Indian monk named Bodhisena helped lead the ceremony to open the giant Buddha statue at Todai-ji temple in Nara.

The seasons matter a great deal. In spring, families picnic under blooming cherry trees, a custom called hanami. On Children's Day, 5 May, families fly carp-shaped streamers called koinobori. Carp swim upstream, so they stand for strength. Poets even capture the seasons in [[@haiku|haiku]], very short poems.

{{story:haiku}}

The same year Granada fell, Queen Isabella and King Ferdinand paid for an Italian sailor, [[@columbus|Christopher Columbus]], to sail west, hoping to reach Asia. He reached the Caribbean instead. Over the next century, Spain conquered huge areas of the Americas, from Mexico to [[@peru|Peru]], with terrible losses for the Indigenous peoples who lived there.

Ships brought back gold and silver, but also new plants: potatoes, [[@food-why-chillies-red|chillies]], maize, chocolate and [[@tomatoes|tomatoes]]. Spanish food was never the same again.

